r 2028"], "rationale": "Arkansas's fundamentals remain firmly Republican, and the 2026 midterms confirmed the pattern: Sanders won a second gubernatorial term and Cotton was re-elected comfortably, even as Democrats showed only localized gains. A slight nudge toward Democrats from those midterm ripples keeps the expected margin near the state's partisan lean, well short of competitive.", "watch": "A national 2028 environment far more favorable to Democrats than 2026, or a strong Democratic nominee making inroads in northwest Arkansas suburbs, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas remains one of the most Republican-leaning states on the presidential map, and the 2026 midterms did little to change that. Sarah Huckabee Sanders won a second term as governor and Tom Cotton was easily re-elected to the Senate, even as Democrats celebrated gains elsewhere in the country. Commentary in the state press notes some 'blue ripples,' largely tied to the Little Rock-based second congressional district, but no statewide breakthrough.\n\nWith no incumbent president on the ballot in 2028 and no candidate-specific polling yet, the forecast rests on Arkansas's strong partisan lean, adjusted slightly for Democratic midterm momentum. Republicans are clear favorites, though the long horizon and absence of data keep uncertainty wide.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0496, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0891, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0911, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0924, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0837, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0872, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem